# Plane-packed (band-interleaved) activation layout: 66/66 exact on silicon, -0.34 ms on YOLOv8s
# Host DESKTOP-CBL5NUA (Desktop 2, Ryzen 7 8700G, XDNA1 Phoenix), 2026-09-20, env mlir-aie-iron
# Device 0. xrt-smi reported "No hardware contexts running on device" before and after every
# run below, and the device was idle at the end.
#
# Supersedes the first version of this log, which concluded "WRONG ON SILICON". That conclusion
# was wrong: the layout and the device were correct and the READBACK was not. Kept visible here
# because the retraction is the useful part.
#
## What it is
#
# Placement.band_rows interleaves a tensor's channel blocks every five rows, so one tile's
# blocks sit adjacent and a k1 or res fill becomes contiguous. That drops the fill from three
# dimensions to two, which is what admits merge_runs on top of merge_quad: each adds one
# dimension and both refuse a four-dimensional pattern, so a 3-D fill gets the quad merge alone
# while k3s2's 2-D one folds 23.6-39.6 packets into a single task
# (results/aie/merge_depth_sizing_20260920.log).
#
# A tensor has ONE layout, so a single wider reader disqualifies it: k3s1 reads eight rows from
# y0-1, k3s2 sixteen from 2*y0-1, pool sixteen from y0-2, k1up2 four from y0>>1, and each
# crosses a band boundary where the row-to-address map stops being affine. 22 of 61 read
# tensors qualify on both models. That is why this is worth a few tenths and not 2 ms.
#
## Correctness
#
#   tools/verify_engine_container.py, yolov8n, --no-workspace-reuse so every layer is readable
#     66/66 layers EXACT, PASS
#
## Latency, same sitting, 300 iterations after 20 warm-up, arms interleaved
#
#   model     arm       real ms   floor ms   compute ms   floor %
#   yolov8n   control     7.371      5.398        1.973    73.2%
#   yolov8n   banded      7.236      5.339        1.897    73.8%
#   yolov8s   control    16.901     12.720        4.181    75.3%
#   yolov8s   banded     16.580     12.474        4.106    75.2%
#   yolov8s   control    16.945     12.775        4.171    75.4%
#   yolov8s   banded     16.588     12.440        4.148    75.0%
#
#   yolov8n  real -0.135 ms            floor -0.059 ms
#   yolov8s  real -0.321 and -0.357    floor -0.246 and -0.335    (mean -0.339 / -0.291)
#
# Predicted -0.108 (yolov8n) and -0.518 (yolov8s) from the descriptor count alone. yolov8n
# lands slightly better than predicted and yolov8s at about two thirds of it, so the per-task
# constant over-predicts on the larger model. Direction and rough size hold; the exact
# coefficient does not, and the measurement is the number to quote.
#
## What it costs
#
#   activation bytes   unchanged (83,072,000 yolov8n / 237,670,400 yolov8s)
#   DMA tasks          2,972 -> 2,896 and 7,143 -> 6,795, exactly as sized
#   insts.bin          430,180 -> 419,476 B and 1,019,140 -> 969,812 B
#   workspace          +5.15% and +5.51%, because a banded and a plane-major tensor of the
#                      same geometry can no longer share a reuse slot
#
## Two constraints found while building it
#
# A shim BD has THREE addressing dimensions plus a repeat, each wrap field ten bits
# (docs/SILICON.md). canonical() folding a whole 6,400 B fill into one 1,600-word run overflows
# that: mlir-aie splits the run, spends a fourth addressing dimension, and the lowering refuses
# the descriptor because the repeat may not be counted in the transfer length. canonical() now
# caps at 1,023 words, and it costs no merging - the task counts are identical either way.
#
# run_drain now canonicalises the drain it builds. Fills arrive at the emitter already folded,
# through merge_quad and merge_runs; a drain went straight to the tap, and on a banded tensor
# its block stride equals its row extent, so the pattern was foldable and a foldable 4-D tap is
# ambiguous about which dimension is the repeat. Folding in the emitter instead was tried and
# REVERTED: it also folds the weight runs' stride-0 replay into a shape the lowering rejects.
#
## The bug, and why the offline suite could not see it
#
# GraphSession.read_tensor carried its own plane-major reshape and never learned band_rows: it
# read blocks * plane_bytes from the tensor base and reshaped [blocks][h][w][8]. Banded, a
# block's rows are not contiguous and the region spans every plane of every band, so that read
# returns the right bytes in the wrong order. _plane_view had been taught banding; this path
# had not, and this is the path the verifier uses. 44/66 exact before the fix, 66/66 after.
#
# The offline suite passed throughout - 256 tests, including layer-exactness against ONNX
# Runtime. It writes an output with write_tensor and reads it with read_tensor, so those agree
# by construction, and the emulation reads activation packets through the same pattern that
# wrote them, so a layout error cancels on both sides. A passing offline test is not evidence
# of on-silicon behaviour, and a readback shared with the thing under test is not a check.
#
## Ruled out on the way, none of them the cause
#
#   BD field overflow    max wrap 200 of 1,023; max step 207,368 of 1,048,575 words; identical
#                        banded and not. DmaPattern validates alignment and dimension count and
#                        NOT field widths, so this was worth checking rather than assuming.
#   drain byte order     building the stream the MemTile join really delivers (core, then block,
#                        then row, then column) and scattering it through o_pattern reproduces
#                        read_tensor on 66 of 66 layers. An earlier probe that fed the pattern
#                        its own indices proved nothing and was discarded.
#   strides              every remaining plane_bytes is a capacity or a reshape, never a stride.
#
## Not established
#
#   - Glass-to-glass was not measured, only NPU dispatch. No comparison against AMD's stack was
#     run in this sitting, and none of these numbers is one.
#   - Only yolov8n was verified layer-by-layer. yolov8s was measured for latency, not checked
#     for exactness.
#   - The +5% workspace was not traced to a slot-by-slot cause, and no attempt was made to
#     recover it by letting banded and plane-major tensors share a slot at different bases.
#   - IGNITE_XDNA_BAND_ROWS=0 restores the old layout exactly and is how both arms were built.
